      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "Prevent OpenCode reset timestamp overflow",
        "body": "## Summary\n- reject non-finite numeric values at the OpenCode and OpenCode Go parser boundary\n- bound computed reset intervals before the trapping `Double`-to-`Int` conversion\n- preserve valid usage percentages and valid quota windows while discarding malformed reset timestamps\n- cover both infinite (`\"1e309\"`) and large finite (`\"1e308\"`) server values in both provider variants\n- add the required changelog entry and contributor credit\n\n## Production-path proof\nThe focused suites call the production OpenCode and OpenCode Go parsers with HTTP-shaped JSON fixtures. For both malformed reset values, the final head preserves rolling usage at 17%, weekly usage at 75%, and the valid weekly reset at 7200 seconds while returning a safe zero for the malformed rolling reset.\n\nA live account probe is not appropriate for this parser boundary: the exact malformed server values cannot be requested from a real account, and repository policy requires fixture coverage rather than reading browser cookies or Keychain data for parser-only behavior.\n\n## Validation\n- `swift test --no-parallel --filter '^CodexBarTests.OpenCodeUsageParserTests/'`: 14 tests passed\n- `swift test --no-parallel --filter '^CodexBarTests.OpenCodeGoUsageParserTests/'`: 28 tests passed\n- `make check`: passed; 145 documentation links, 21 site locales, 0 Swift format/lint violations\n- `make test`: all 46/46 shards passed\n- autoreview on the final branch diff: clean, 0.86 correctness\n\n## Risk\nLow. The change is confined to numeric parsing and reset-duration conversion for OpenCode variants. Invalid reset metadata degrades to the existing zero-reset representation; valid usage and reset data remain covered.\n",
